// orelim-branch-scope — the lemma set the OR-ELIM BRANCH closer searches over
// (ruResidualOrElim, refiners.flow). This FILE takes precedence over the built-in
// fallback list; edit it without a rebuild.
//
// WHY ITS OWN NARROW SCOPE. A branch sub-prove is a SMALL problem — the residual
// goal plus one added disjunct — but it is run by a bare bfs, so the scope must be
// tight: handing the branch the caller's full stage scope made the bfs EXPLODE and
// lose t19/t22 (measured). What it needs is the glue that turns a case assumption
// into the goal, not the whole theory.
//
// THE INFERENCE-FORM LESSON (measured twice). Backward bfs reduces a goal by
// matching a rule's CONCLUSION, so it needs the *i-form* of each schema, not the
// implication form:
//   - ∨-introduction: orc/olc are `φ→(φ∨ψ)` and are USELESS backward; orci/olci
//     (`φ ⊢ φ∨ψ`) reduce the goal in one step. Without them t22's branch failed.
//   - ⊆-transitivity: sstr left the middle term unbound; sstri/sseqtri chain from
//     the pool. Without them t73's branch failed.
//
// FAMILIES:
//   equality → ⊆ / ∧ / ∨ glue   the case assumption is usually an equality or a
//                               membership, the goal a ⊆/∨ combination
//   ⊆-schemata (0-hyp)          the inclusion facts a branch transports along;
//                               same family as relnorm-scope's difss/difdifss
//   composers                   ax-mp / syl / mpbi and the ∧,∨ projections

// equality → ⊆, and the ∧/∨ intro-eliminations
eqimss eqimss2 eqss eqssi eqcomi jca simpli simpri olci orci

// ⊆-transitivity and equality-transport along ⊆ (I-FORMS — see the lesson above)
sstr sstri sseqtri sseqtrri sseq1 sseq2

// 0-hyp ⊆-schemata the branches transport along (difsssymdif/symdifcom carry the
// t73 class: x ⊆ (A∖B) ⊆ (A△B), and the △-commuted second branch)
difss ssun1 ssun2 difsssymdif symdifcom velpw

// composers
ax-mp mpbi mpbir syl biimpi biimpri
